## Target shape

```go
// withRLSVar runs fn in a transaction with a single GUC set to scope RLS.
// Private — callers use WithOrgID / WithUserID.
func withRLSVar(ctx context.Context, pool *pgxpool.Pool, varName, varValue string, fn func(pgx.Tx) error) error {
    tx, err := pool.Begin(ctx)
    if err != nil { return fmt.Errorf("begin tx: %w", err) }
    defer tx.Rollback(ctx) //nolint:errcheck
    if _, err := tx.Exec(ctx, "SELECT set_config($1, $2, true)", varName, varValue); err != nil {
        return fmt.Errorf("set %s: %w", varName, err)
    }
    if err := fn(tx); err != nil { return err }
    return tx.Commit(ctx)
}

func WithOrgID(ctx, pool, orgID, fn) error  { return withRLSVar(ctx, pool, "app.current_org_id", orgID, fn) }
func WithUserID(ctx, pool, userID, fn) error { return withRLSVar(ctx, pool, "app.current_user_id", userID, fn) }
```

## Important — verify the parameterised set_config form is safe

Originally `db.WithOrgID` hardcoded the var name into the SQL literal (`SELECT set_config('app.current_org_id', $1, true)`). The new helper uses BOTH names as parameters (`$1` and `$2`). Postgres' `set_config` accepts parameterised names — verify with a quick test (the existing `internal/db/migrate_test.go` or a fresh one). If for any reason set_config doesn't accept a parameterised first argument, fall back to building the SQL string from a vetted allow-list of var names rather than using string interpolation.

